hybrid-x86-oracle — licensing
=============================

This project's OWN source code — everything authored in this repository:
`src/`, `csrc/`, `sail/`, `build.rs`, `scripts/`, the test suite, and the
documentation — is licensed under the BSD 3-Clause License (below).

`patches/` is the one exception: those files modify third-party sources, so
each inherits the license of what it patches (today: `patches/bochs/*.patch`
is LGPL-2.1-or-later, being a modification of Bochs).

It also BUNDLES and statically links several third-party components under their
own licenses; see "Third-party components" at the end. Two of them are copyleft:
the bundled GMP arithmetic (mini-gmp, LGPL/GPL) in every build, and the Bochs
CPU sources (LGPL-2.1-or-later) when the `bochs` feature is on. A DISTRIBUTED
BINARY of this crate is therefore a combined work subject to those terms as
well. The accurate combined SPDX expression is:

    BSD-3-Clause AND BSD-2-Clause AND MIT
      AND Apache-2.0
      AND LGPL-2.1-or-later
      AND (LGPL-3.0-or-later OR GPL-2.0-or-later)

Which terms a given build actually carries:

  * a default build carries BSD-3-Clause, BSD-2-Clause, MIT and the mini-gmp
    (LGPL-3.0-or-later OR GPL-2.0-or-later) term;
  * `--features bochs` adds LGPL-2.1-or-later;
  * `--features kvm` adds Apache-2.0. Rust dependencies are statically linked
    like anything else, and while kvm-ioctls, libc and windows-sys are all
    "Apache-2.0 OR MIT" — electing MIT keeps them inside the MIT term above —
    kvm-bindings is Apache-2.0 only, so that term is unavoidable in a KVM build.
    `--features whp` adds nothing beyond MIT.

Those terms coexist because the copyleft ones can be taken at version 3:
Apache-2.0 is incompatible with GPL-2.0-only, but Bochs is LGPL-2.1-OR-LATER
and mini-gmp offers LGPL-3.0-or-later, so a build that mixes Apache-2.0 with
either must exercise that "or later" option.

